# EdgeForge

> **Work in progress. All numbers in this repository are interim until the final audit by the lead.**
> Audited research prototype, not a product. Independent, non-commercial portfolio project. Nothing here is a claim of commercial use, of a betting edge, or of a deployed product.

## What this shows

1. **Measured:** a results-only Dixon-Coles model against the Pinnacle closing line on {{a_n_matches}} matches (2024/25), and props, same-game accumulators, lineup shocks and in-play models on one StatsBomb season (2015/16).
2. **What failed to beat the market:** 1X2 log loss {{a_model_ll}} against {{a_market_ll}}; weight in a pool with the market {{a_enc_weight}} (95% CI {{a_enc_lo}} to {{a_enc_hi}}); {{a_repl_total}} confirmatory findings, {{a_repl_n}} replicated, {{a_repl_not}} did not.
3. **What it is:** an audited research prototype of a football sportsbook pricing engine, every number rendered from metrics files; not a betting system, not deployed, no edge claimed.

A football sportsbook pricing engine built as the lifecycle of a price across three information states: **opening** (about two days before kick-off, no lineups), **lineups** (confirmed XI and bench, one hour before) and **in-play** (score and dismissals up to the minute). Football only.

Every number on this page is rendered from `artifacts/metrics/` by `uv run edgeforge docs render`; `uv run edgeforge docs check` and the test suite fail if a document disagrees with its metrics file. Each metrics file carries its git sha, data version, command, config hash and seed.

## What was found (the negative results come first)

- **The pre-match model does not beat the market.** On {{a_n_matches}} Premier League, Bundesliga, La Liga, Serie A and Ligue 1 matches (2024/25) the Dixon-Coles model has a 1X2 log loss of {{a_model_ll}} against {{a_market_ll}} for the Pinnacle closing line (de-vigged proportionally). Its weight in a log-linear pool with the market is {{a_enc_weight}} (95% CI {{a_enc_lo}} to {{a_enc_hi}}): no detectable information beyond the market (the interval reaches {{a_enc_hi}}).
- **The closing line does not move toward the model** (comparison with an early snapshot taken roughly one to three days before kick-off, not timestamped; D-024): slope {{a_move_slope}} (95% CI {{a_move_lo}} to {{a_move_hi}}).
- **No replicated inefficiency.** {{a_repl_total}} confirmatory findings, {{a_repl_n}} replicated, {{a_repl_not}} did not: of the {{a_repl_total}} pre-registered findings that survived the multiple-testing control in the confirmatory season, {{a_repl_n}} reproduce in the replication season (same sign, p below one in twenty, uncorrected; the replication season is measured against the market-average close because Pinnacle prices stop in early 2026), and those are model-versus-market gaps (the market is better); the Bundesliga home-win bias does not replicate (p = {{a_d1_repl_p}}). The favourite-longshot slope at the close is {{a_flb_slope}} (95% CI {{a_flb_lo}} to {{a_flb_hi}}), compatible with a calibrated market. These statements concern a results-only Dixon-Coles model compared with the Pinnacle closing line; they are not a proof that the market is efficient. Power, taken only from the intervals already reported: the 95% interval of the pool weight has a half-width of {{a_enc_half}}, that of the line-movement slope {{a_move_half}} and that of the favourite-longshot slope {{a_flb_half}}, so effects of about that size or smaller cannot be excluded on this sample.
- **Player props:** {{p4_props_better}} of six markets beat a rolling per-90 baseline after multiple-testing control (anytime scorer: log-loss difference {{p4_anytime_diff}}, negative is better); shots 1+ is a null ({{p4_shots1_diff}}). No historical prop odds exist, so props are evaluated on probability quality only.
- **Same-game accumulators:** with templates fixed before any test result, the simulated joint beats the naive product of the legs in {{j_better}} of {{j_n_tests}} template-by-state tests and loses in {{j_worse}}; pooled log loss {{j_sim_ll}} against {{j_naive_ll}} on {{j_n_instances}} settled instances, and calibration error {{j_sim_ece}} against {{j_naive_ece}}. Both prices use the simulator's own marginals, so the comparison isolates dependence, and the named players are each team's mechanically chosen top scorers. The simulator still under-predicts some multi-player templates (the five-leg template T10: {{j_t10_sim}} predicted, {{j_t10_rate}} realised). A match-level frailty meant to fix this was tested and not adopted. A stronger baseline, a Gaussian copula with the same marginals and one correlation per template fitted on the tuning window (D-063), reaches {{cp_share}} of the simulator's log-loss gain over the naive product: pooled lineups log loss {{cp_ll_sim}} simulated, {{cp_ll_cop}} copula, {{cp_ll_naive}} naive product. The simulated joint is better than the copula in {{cp_s_better}} of {{cp_s_cells}} template-by-state tests and worse in {{cp_s_worse}} (pooled difference {{cp_diff}}, 95% CI {{cp_diff_lo}} to {{cp_diff_hi}}), and the copula is better calibrated overall (error {{cp_ece_cop}} against {{cp_ece_sim}}). Most of the benefit over the naive product is therefore generic dependence between the legs, not the detailed simulator.
- **Lineup information (Pillar C, an upper bound):** {{c_shock_rot}} of rotation-risk players' prop prices move by more than a quarter once the lineups are known, against {{c_shock_nailed}} for nailed starters. In a simulation, a bettor who knows the lineups earns an expected {{c_inf5}} units per settled proposition at a five per cent margin (noise floor {{c_ctrl5}}). On the stricter per-offered-proposition basis of Phase 6b, at a ten per cent overround, settling props void-if-not-starting with odds-ratio margin allocation cuts the residual to {{b_reco}} from {{b_base}} (at five per cent: {{b_reco5}} from {{b_base5}}); the two bases are not comparable. These are simulations, not observed betting.
- **In-play:** the model beats the naive in-play baseline in {{i_better}} of {{i_n_tests}} checkpoint-by-market tests and loses in {{i_worse}} (the final-1X2 log-loss difference after a dismissal is {{i_dismiss}}, 95% CI {{i_dismiss_lo}} to {{i_dismiss_hi}}, on {{i_dismiss_n}} propositions; negative means the model is better; the baseline is naive). Both still under-predict remaining goals ({{i_rem_model}} model, {{i_rem_base}} baseline, {{i_rem_obs}} observed). **No in-play market prices exist in the data, so the in-play model cannot be compared with a market.** That naive baseline ignores the score. Against a stronger baseline with a time profile and a score-state multiplier but no dismissal terms and no Dixon-Coles input (B1, pre-registered as family IV in D-063), the in-play model is better in {{b1_better}} cells, worse in {{b1_worse}} and not distinguishable in {{b1_nd}} of {{b1_cells}}; pooled final-1X2 log loss {{b1_ll_pb}} for the in-play model, {{b1_ll_b1}} for B1 and {{b1_ll_naive}} for the naive baseline. Most of the advantage over the naive baseline therefore comes from the time profile and the score state, not from the extra model terms. The in-play result rests on one season: {{sb_cov_n}} league seasons of the StatsBomb open data were checked for a sealed second-season holdout and {{sb_cov_eligible}} qualifies (the other seasons cover single clubs, D-064), so no replication on a second season could be built.
- **Forward shadow log (pre-match only, no betting):** the first batch (the Premier League weekend of 6 to 8 November 2026) was committed and pushed before kick-off: {{sh_logged}} logged, {{sh_settled}} settled, against the market-average close because the current football-data files carry no Pinnacle prices (D-059), a weaker benchmark than the Pinnacle close used above; a record is only evidence of timing as far as the push timestamp on the remote. Model 1X2 log loss {{sh_ll_model}} against {{sh_ll_market}} for the market-average closing line (model minus market {{sh_ll_diff}}, bootstrap 95% CI {{sh_ll_diff_lo}} to {{sh_ll_diff_hi}}); {{sh_cold_n}} of the settled fixtures involve a promoted team with little history and are reported as a separate segment. {{sh_settled}} matches show the process working, not an edge: nothing can be concluded. Verdict: {{sh_verdict}}. The second batch (21 to 23 November 2026) also carries {{sh_v2_logged}} records of a forward-only candidate model version that adds shots on target (D-065, D-066); it is never evaluated on any old window and nothing is settled yet.

## What is not claimed

No betting edge, no profit, no deployed or commercial use, no live feed, no in-play player props. Props and SGAs are validated on realised outcomes, never against prop odds. All backtests are on the 2015/16 StatsBomb season (props, SGAs, lineup shock, in-play) or the 2024/25 football-data season (pre-match market comparison).

## Known process violations (disclosed)

The cold-start prior grid and the player rolling-baseline grid were widened after their test numbers had been seen, and calibrators were first adopted on the test window; the rules D-040 and D-043 fix this from Phase 4 on and the affected results are labelled in `docs/GATES.md` and `docs/DECISIONS.md` (D-039, D-040, D-043). The rolling baseline is the comparison for the player-prop results above.

## Quick start

```
uv sync
uv run edgeforge --help
uv run edgeforge serve build     # derived serving bundle from the local warehouse
uv run edgeforge serve api       # FastAPI on http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs
uv run edgeforge serve app       # Streamlit trader app on http://localhost:8501
uv run edgeforge shadow predict --fixtures fixtures.csv --commit   # pre-match shadow log
uv run edgeforge shadow settle
uv run edgeforge docs render && uv run edgeforge docs check
uv run edgeforge ci-local        # fresh-clone lint, types and tests
```

Tests and lint run via a scripted fresh-clone check (`uv run edgeforge ci-local`, evidence in `artifacts/metrics/ci_local.json`; last run at commit `{{ci_sha}}`, {{ci_passed}} tests passed); the GitHub Actions workflow is included but not executed (account limitation). Container files and Cloud Run scripts are prepared in `Dockerfile` and `deploy/`; the image was not built here (the Docker engine was unavailable; a fresh-clone run with production-only dependencies was checked instead) and nothing is deployed.

## Where things are

`docs/DECISIONS.md` (every locked decision and pre-registration), `docs/GATES.md` (audited phase reports), `reports/technical_report.md`, `reports/evidence_pack.md` (what a sceptic can verify, and how), `experiments/registry.jsonl` (every experiment including the rejected ones), `artifacts/metrics/` (all numbers, with provenance).

